            <ArticleTitle>Office-based management of adult-acquired flatfoot deformity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>291-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.mcna.2013.10.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0025-7125(13)00154-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adult-acquired flatfoot deformity is associated with dysfunction of the posterior tibial tendon, leading to loss of the medial arch. Patients tend to present with medial pain and swelling, but later in the disease process can also present with lateral-sided pain. The mainstay of nonoperative treatment is nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, weight loss, and orthotic insoles or brace use. The goals of therapy are to provide relief of symptoms and prevent progression of the deformity. If nonoperative management fails, a variety of surgical procedures are available; however, these require a lengthy recovery, and therefore patients should be advised accordingly.</AbstractText>
